# install.sh on a machine that has never seen this repository -- 2026-10-02

Reproduce: `scripts/install-sh-in-docker.sh`
Host: Ubuntu 22.04 x86_64, Docker 29.8.1, container image `debian:bookworm-slim`.
Commit under test: the working tree whose `scripts/install.sh` is byte-identical to
`scripts/install.sh` at `3ceac9b1` (verified with `cmp` against `git show
3ceac9b1:scripts/install.sh`).
Scope: whether the command the README puts on its front page installs a verified
binary on a clean Linux machine, and whether the two integrity checks it claims to
perform are actually performed.

## Why this was not already known

The installers had only been checked by
`scripts/ci/test-installer-signature-policy.py`, which reads the script as text and
asserts that certain error strings exist in it. Text assertions cannot tell you that
the function containing them is never called, and one of them was not.

`scripts/install.sh` defined `verify_checksum()` -- fetch `SHA256SUMS`, find the entry
for the asset, compare, exit 1 on mismatch -- and had no call site for it. Before the
fix, `git show 73a9d7c8:scripts/install.sh | grep -n 'verify_checksum\|verify_signature'`
printed:

    478:verify_checksum() {          <- definition
    521:verify_signature() {
    585:verify_signature             <- the only integrity check that runs

so `curl -fsSL .../install.sh | bash` downloaded the ~160 MB artifact, printed nothing
about the published digest, and installed. Every error path inside the function
(`could not fetch SHA256SUMS`, `SHA256SUMS has no entry for`, `checksum mismatch`, and
the `checksum OK` success line) was unreachable, and the comment above it said the
opposite of the truth. `install.ps1` and `install.bat` do the same work inline, so
only the sh installer was affected.

## Run 1: what the clean-machine run reported

`scripts/install-sh-in-docker.sh` installs into a stock `debian:bookworm-slim`
container whose only added packages are `curl`, `python3`, `python3-cryptography` --
no cargo, no repository, no prior `~/.chaos`, and no `CHAOS_SIGNING_PUBLIC_KEY` passed
in. Two checks failed:

    FAILED no 'checksum OK' line: the published SHA256SUMS was not consulted
    FAILED a blank key did not fail fast (exit 0); output was: Chaos installer ...

The first is the defect above. The second was a bug in the harness, and it mattered
more than it looks:

    install_out=$(in_container sh -c "CHAOS_SIGNING_PUBLIC_KEY=\"\"; bash ... ")

`VAR=x; cmd` assigns a shell variable and then runs the command *without* it, so
neither fail-closed control ever reached the installer. The blank-key run used the
built-in key and exited 0; the foreign-key control, which was next in line and had not
run yet, would have done the same -- i.e. the two checks that exist to prove the
signature line is not vacuous were themselves vacuous. `run_install` now goes through
`env` (`env CHAOS_SIGNING_PUBLIC_KEY= bash ...`), and the script refuses to continue
until a probe confirms the controls can reach the child's environment:

    ok  the controls really do set CHAOS_SIGNING_PUBLIC_KEY in the child's environment

## Fixes

1. `verify_checksum` is called before `verify_signature`, both before `chmod +x`.
2. `scripts/ci/test-installer-signature-policy.py` asserts structurally that a
   definition is not enough: each of `verify_checksum` / `verify_signature` must appear
   exactly once as a top-level call, in that order, and before `chmod +x "$TMP"`.
   Mutation-verified -- deleting the call line makes the test fail with
   `verify_checksum must be called exactly once at the top level (found 0 call sites)`,
   and restoring it makes the suite pass (7 tests).

## Run 2: 19 checks, all passing

    == the machine doing the installing
       ok  container is up: Linux 6.8.0-138-generic x86_64
       ok  prerequisites install from distro packages: curl, python3, python3-cryptography
           python3 3.11.2, cryptography 38.0.4
       ok  no prior install to inherit: no ~/.chaos, no chaos on PATH, no cargo
       ok  the built-in key is the repository's CHAOS_SIGNING_PUBLIC_KEY

    == the command README tells users to run
       ok  install.sh exited 0 with no CHAOS_SIGNING_PUBLIC_KEY in the environment
       ok  the digest was checked: checksum OK (0ee7d6ee3c92159d2b013c7d081cf2cc265d2d59a808ebb0fb9f2554a7b4bfa2)
       ok  the signature was checked: signature OK

    == what landed on disk
       ok  the artifact is stored under the versioned name auto-update expects: /root/.chaos/downloads/chaos-0.4.2-linux-x86_64
       ok  bin/chaos -> ../downloads/chaos-0.4.2-linux-x86_64 (relative, so it survives a bind-mount that remaps $HOME)
       ok  bin/chaos resolves and is executable
       ok  bin/agent points at the same artifact
       ok  downloads/chaos-latest names the current version
       ok  the installed binary runs and reports: chaos 0.4.2 (8503250745ff)
       ok  PATH was configured in a shell rc

    == a second run
       ok  re-running is a no-op: already installed: chaos 0.4.2 (8503250745ff)

    == controls: the signature check has to be able to say no
       ok  the controls really do set CHAOS_SIGNING_PUBLIC_KEY in the child's environment
       ok  a blank key is refused before any download starts
       ok  a valid-but-foreign key is refused with 'signature verification FAILED'
       ok  the refused install left the working artifact untouched (0ee7d6ee3c92159d2b013c7d081cf2cc265d2d59a808ebb0fb9f2554a7b4bfa2)

    all 19 check(s) passed

Two details worth keeping. The foreign-key run prints `checksum OK` and *then*
refuses, which is the intended order -- the digest catches a corrupt or swapped
transfer, the signature is what establishes provenance, and neither is load-bearing
alone. And the digest above is the same `0ee7d6ee…a7b4bfa2` that
`scripts/verify-release-signature.sh --tag v0.4.2` recomputed independently from the
release page (see `release-signature-v0.4.2-2026-10-02.log`), so the installer and that
script are looking at the same bytes.

## Cross-checks after the run

- `cmp` says the script that ran in the container is identical to
  `scripts/install.sh` at `3ceac9b1`, so the transcript describes committed content.
- `https://raw.githubusercontent.com/chao2hang/chaos-code/main/scripts/install.sh`
  is now byte-identical to the working tree. During run 1 it was not (that run printed
  a note about it), because `main` still had the version without the checksum call:
  the one-liner a reader copies from the README was behind the fix until `3ceac9b1`
  was pushed.
- The `built-in key is the repository's CHAOS_SIGNING_PUBLIC_KEY` check reads the
  Actions variable with `gh variable get` and compares it to
  `DEFAULT_SIGNING_PUBLIC_KEY` in the script under test, so the key the installer ships
  and the key the release signs against cannot drift apart silently.

## What this file is NOT evidence about

- `install.ps1` and `install.bat`. Nothing has executed either one. Separately found
  in this round: `install.ps1` did not even parse (`The Try statement is missing its
  Catch or Finally block`) since `21f5a186`; fixed in `4d3eb266` and now gated by
  `scripts/ci/check-powershell-syntax.py`. Parsing is not installing.
- macOS, or Linux on arm64. The container was x86_64 Debian and picked
  `chaos-linux-x64`.
- The mirror paths. The run used the origin URL; `CHAOS_GITHUB_MIRROR`, `CHAOS_CN=1`
  and the public fallback list were not exercised.
- The escape hatches. `CHAOS_SKIP_CHECKSUM=1` and `CHAOS_SKIP_SIGNATURE=1` were not
  exercised -- the run asserts the opposite, that no skip line appeared.
- The upgrade path over an existing install of a *different* version, or a
  pre-existing `~/.chaos` written by an older release. The container started clean and
  the second run was the same version, i.e. a no-op.
- That `chaos update` afterwards verifies and installs a newer release. That is
  `docs/verification/updater-feed-e2e-2026-10-02.log` (loopback feed) and
  `docs/verification/release-signature-v0.4.2-2026-10-02.log` (real bytes, real key),
  each with its own limits.
- Version currency: `v0.4.2` is the newest published release; the repository is ahead
  of it. Re-run against the new tag after the next release.
